/*  Where the apples are.

    ★★ A FIXED WALK, NOT A RANDOM ONE, and that is the oracle's doing rather than a simplification.
    This project holds the two backends to byte-for-byte agreement, and `chance` cannot give it:
    the interpreter's generator is .NET's xoshiro256** and the compiler emits its own xorshift64*,
    a documented fork. A random apple would put this game in the skip list beside `markov.cufe`,
    where the only thing left to check is that it builds.

    ★ It costs the game less than it sounds. A snake game is hard because the SNAKE is in the way,
    not because the apple is a surprise — and a fixed course is what a speedrun is played on. What
    is lost is replay variety; what is kept is every other test in the corpus.

    ⚠ The apple may never sit under the snake, so the walk skips whatever the snake covers. With a
    field of 160 squares and a snake of a few, that search ends immediately; it is written as a
    loop anyway because the one time it matters is the one time nobody is watching.
*/

/// The places apples appear, in the order they appear in.
///
/// ★ Spread across the field on purpose: an apple near the last one makes a game of small
/// wiggles, and one on the far side makes a game of crossing your own tail, which is the one
/// that is worth playing.
///
/// ★★ The FIRST one sits straight ahead of the opening snake, which is a decision about the
/// unattended run as much as about the player. Nobody steers a corpus run, so an apple anywhere
/// else would have the game print ten frames of a snake walking into a wall and never once show
/// what eating does — the growth path would be dead code that type-checks.
Bind series of spot to orchard:
    Define places as a series of spot.
    Insert cast at on (4, 14) into places.
    Insert cast at on (6, 4) into places.
    Insert cast at on (3, 18) into places.
    Insert cast at on (7, 11) into places.
    Insert cast at on (1, 6) into places.
    Insert cast at on (5, 16) into places.
    Return places.
Done.

/// The apple to aim at, given how many have been eaten already.
Bind spot to apple-for, given (the worm which, the number eaten):
    Define places as cast orchard.
    Define how-many as the number of places.

    Define tried as 0.
    While tried is less than how-many, repeat:
        Define picked as item (((eaten + tried) % how-many) + 1) of places.
        If not cast which's covers on (picked), return picked.
        Increment tried by 1.
    Done.

    /* Every apple is under the snake, which means the snake is nearly the whole field.
       Handing back the head is harmless: it is eaten at once, and the next one will be free. */
    Return cast which's head.
Done.
